(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("llm",{"llm/what-an-llm-is-and-how-it-predicts-tokens":{"html":"\u003cp>A large language model, or LLM, is a program trained on a very large amount of text to predict what comes next. That one skill, repeated thousands of times, produces answers, code, summaries and conversation. Engineers who understand the prediction loop make better decisions about prompts, costs and safety than those who treat the model as a mind or as a search engine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The prediction loop: text in, one token out, repeat\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why the same prompt can give different answers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What models are good at, and where they fail\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A mental model you can use when something goes wrong\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-loop\">The loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The model receives text, converted to numbers, and produces a score for every possible next piece of text. One piece is chosen, added to the input, and the process repeats until the model produces a stop signal or hits a length limit. Nothing else happens inside: no lookup in a database and no running of code unless you give it tools. A reply of 300 words is hundreds of small predictions in a row, each depending on everything before it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-it-feels-smart\">Why it feels smart\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Predicting text well requires capturing grammar, facts, style, reasoning patterns and code structure that appear in the training text. So the model can explain a stack trace, translate a paragraph or write a Terraform module, because those patterns were common in what it learned. It does not &quot;look up&quot; an answer. It produces the most plausible continuation, shaped by training to be helpful and honest.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"strengths-and-weak-spots\">Strengths and weak spots\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Strong\u003c/strong>: summarising, rewriting, classifying, extracting fields from messy text, drafting code and explanations, translating between formats.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Weaker\u003c/strong>: exact arithmetic on long numbers, counting characters, facts after its training cutoff, rarely seen details (an obscure flag of a tool), and anything that needs real-time data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Risky\u003c/strong>: confident but wrong answers, called hallucinations. A fluent sentence is not evidence of a true one.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The practical rule: use the model for language and pattern work, and use tools (a calculator, a search, a database) for exact facts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-mental-model-that-helps-debugging\">A mental model that helps debugging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a result is wrong, ask three questions. \u003cstrong>What did the model actually see?\u003c/strong> Print the exact input, including the system prompt and any retrieved text. \u003cstrong>Is the answer in the input?\u003c/strong> If not, the model must guess. \u003cstrong>Is the task a language task?\u003c/strong> If it needs exactness, add a tool or a check. Most &quot;model problems&quot; are input problems. Keep the model&#39;s output as untrusted data: validate it before it reaches a database, a shell or a customer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You ask a model for the exact SHA-256 of a string and it answers with a plausible 64-character hash. Why is this unreliable?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The model predicts plausible text; it does not run the hash algorithm. Use a tool or code for exact computation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Language task versus exact task.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How What an LLM is and how it predicts tokens fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The loop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Why it feels smart\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Strengths and weak spots\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A mental model that helps debugging\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The loop to Why it feels smart; 2, Why it feels smart to Strengths and weak spots; 3, Strengths and weak spots to A mental model that helps debugging.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The loop \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The model receives text, converted to numbers, and produces a score for every possible next piece of text.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Asking the model for exact arithmetic or recent facts without tools.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Why it feels smart \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Predicting text well requires capturing grammar, facts, style, reasoning patterns and code structure that appear in the training text.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not looking at the exact input the model received.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Strengths and weak spots \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Strong: summarising, rewriting, classifying, extracting fields from messy text, drafting code and explanations, translating between formats.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Putting model output straight into a shell or database.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>A mental model that helps debugging \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> When a result is wrong, ask three questions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Treating fluent output as verified output.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-confident-wrong-flag\">Incident: The confident wrong flag\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An on-call engineer pasted a model answer into a runbook without checking it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl rollout restart deploy/web --graceful-period=30\nkubectl rollout restart --help | grep -i grace\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout restart deploy/web --graceful-period=30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: unknown flag: --graceful-period\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout restart --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">help\u003c/span> | grep -i grace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(no output)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The model had produced a flag that looks right but does not exist for that command. The runbook was corrected using the tool&#39;s own help, and the team added a rule: commands from a model are tested in a safe environment and checked against the official reference before they enter documentation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating fluent output as verified output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Asking the model for exact arithmetic or recent facts without tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not looking at the exact input the model received\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting model output straight into a shell or database\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-loop","text":"The loop"},{"id":"why-it-feels-smart","text":"Why it feels smart"},{"id":"strengths-and-weak-spots","text":"Strengths and weak spots"},{"id":"a-mental-model-that-helps-debugging","text":"A mental model that helps debugging"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-confident-wrong-flag","text":"Incident: The confident wrong flag"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Models overview","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/about-claude/models/overview"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Glossary","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/resources/glossary"}],"text":"A large language model, or LLM, is a program trained on a very large amount of text to predict what comes next. That one skill, repeated thousands of times, produces answers, code, summaries and conversation. Engineers who understand the prediction loop make better decisions about prompts, costs and safety than those who treat the model as a mind or as a search engine. What you will learn The prediction loop: text in, one token out, repeat Why the same prompt can give different answers What models are good at, and where they fail A mental model you can use when something goes wrong The loop The model receives text, converted to numbers, and produces a score for every possible next piece of text. One piece is chosen, added to the input, and the process repeats until the model produces a stop signal or hits a length limit. Nothing else happens inside: no lookup in a database and no running of code unless you give it tools. A reply of 300 words is hundreds of small predictions in a row, each depending on everything before it. Why it feels smart Predicting text well requires capturing grammar, facts, style, reasoning patterns and code structure that appear in the training text. So the model can explain a stack trace, translate a paragraph or write a Terraform module, because those patterns were common in what it learned. It does not \"look up\" an answer. It produces the most plausible continuation, shaped by training to be helpful and honest. Strengths and weak spots Strong : summarising, rewriting, classifying, extracting fields from messy text, drafting code and explanations, translating between formats. Weaker : exact arithmetic on long numbers, counting characters, facts after its training cutoff, rarely seen details (an obscure flag of a tool), and anything that needs real-time data. Risky : confident but wrong answers, called hallucinations. A fluent sentence is not evidence of a true one. The practical rule: use the model for language and pattern work, and use tools (a calculator, a search, a database) for exact facts. A mental model that helps debugging When a result is wrong, ask three questions. What did the model actually see? Print the exact input, including the system prompt and any retrieved text. Is the answer in the input? If not, the model must guess. Is the task a language task? If it needs exactness, add a tool or a check. Most \"model problems\" are input problems. Keep the model&#39;s output as untrusted data: validate it before it reaches a database, a shell or a customer. Predict first. You ask a model for the exact SHA-256 of a string and it answers with a plausible 64-character hash. Why is this unreliable? Your guess Answer: The model predicts plausible text; it does not run the hash algorithm. Use a tool or code for exact computation. Language task versus exact task. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How What an LLM is and how it predicts tokens fits together Flow in order: 1, The loop to Why it feels smart; 2, Why it feels smart to Strengths and weak spots; 3, Strengths and weak spots to A mental model that helps debugging. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The loop Step What it does: The model receives text, converted to numbers, and produces a score for every possible next piece of text. If it fails or is weak: Asking the model for exact arithmetic or recent facts without tools. Why it feels smart Step What it does: Predicting text well requires capturing grammar, facts, style, reasoning patterns and code structure that appear in the training text. If it fails or is weak: Not looking at the exact input the model received. Strengths and weak spots Step What it does: Strong: summarising, rewriting, classifying, extracting fields from messy text, drafti"},"llm/tokens-context-windows-and-limits":{"html":"\u003cp>Models do not read characters or words. They read tokens, small pieces of text, and every limit and every price is counted in tokens. Knowing what a token is and what fills the context window explains why long chats get worse, why prices depend on length, and why a prompt that works on a short example can fail on real data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a token is, and rough sizes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What counts toward the context window\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Input limits versus output limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Strategies for text that does not fit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tokens\">Tokens\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A tokenizer splits text into pieces such as whole common words, parts of rare words, punctuation and spaces. As a rule of thumb, one token is about four characters of English text, or three quarters of a word. Code, other languages and unusual strings often need more tokens per character. Different model families use different tokenizers, so counts differ. Use the provider&#39;s token counting tool rather than guessing when limits matter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-context-window\">The context window\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The context window is the total number of tokens the model can consider in one request: the system prompt, the conversation so far, any documents or tool results you include, and the answer being generated. When the sum is too large, the request fails or older text must be dropped. Long contexts also cost more and can reduce accuracy for details buried in the middle, so more text is not always better.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"input-and-output-limits\">Input and output limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Providers set a maximum number of output tokens per reply, separate from the context size. If you ask for a long report with a small output limit, the answer is cut off, and the response says it stopped because of length. Always check the stop reason in the response. A truncated JSON document is invalid JSON, which breaks the program that parses it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"response = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=1024,\n    messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: prompt}],\n)\nif response.stop_reason == &quot;max_tokens&quot;:\n    raise RuntimeError(&quot;answer was cut off; raise max_tokens or shorten the task&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">response = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1024\u003c/span>,\n    messages=[{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;role&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;content&quot;\u003c/span>: prompt}],\n)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> response.stop_reason == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;max_tokens&quot;\u003c/span>:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> RuntimeError(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;answer was cut off; raise max_tokens or shorten the task&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-text-does-not-fit\">When text does not fit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Select\u003c/strong>: send only relevant parts, found by search or by simple rules.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Summarise\u003c/strong>: compress old conversation turns into a short summary.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Chunk\u003c/strong>: process a long document in pieces and combine the results.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cache\u003c/strong>: reuse a large, unchanging prefix, such as instructions or a reference document, using prompt caching to cut cost and delay.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Measure\u003c/strong>: log input and output token counts for each request, so cost and growth are visible.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A chat application sends the full history every turn. After 40 turns the answers get slower and costlier. Why, and what do you change?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Every turn resends all earlier tokens, so input grows each time. Summarise or trim old turns and cache stable parts.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Input tokens add up.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Tokens, context windows and limits fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Tokens\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The context window\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Input and output limits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">When text does not fit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Tokens to The context window; 2, The context window to Input and output limits; 3, Input and output limits to When text does not fit.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Tokens \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A tokenizer splits text into pieces such as whole common words, parts of rare words, punctuation and spaces.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Guessing token counts instead of measuring.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>The context window \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The context window is the total number of tokens the model can consider in one request: the system prompt, the conversation so far, any documents or tool results you...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Putting entire documents in when a few paragraphs would do.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Input and output limits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Providers set a maximum number of output tokens per reply, separate from the context size.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring the stop reason.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>When text does not fit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Select: send only relevant parts, found by search or by simple rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Resending unlimited history.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-report-that-stopped-in-the-middle\">Incident: The report that stopped in the middle\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly job that asked a model for a JSON report failed to parse its output.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python summarize.py\npython -c &quot;print(resp.stop_reason, resp.usage.output_tokens)&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python summarize.py\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">json.decoder.JSONDecodeError: Unterminated string starting at: line 41 column 12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;print(resp.stop_reason, resp.usage.output_tokens)&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">max_tokens 1024\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The model hit the output limit in the middle of the JSON. The fix raised max_tokens, asked for shorter field values, and added a check that raises an error when the stop reason is max_tokens instead of passing broken text to the parser.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Guessing token counts instead of measuring\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the stop reason\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resending unlimited history\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting entire documents in when a few paragraphs would do\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"tokens","text":"Tokens"},{"id":"the-context-window","text":"The context window"},{"id":"input-and-output-limits","text":"Input and output limits"},{"id":"when-text-does-not-fit","text":"When text does not fit"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-report-that-stopped-in-the-middle","text":"Incident: The report that stopped in the middle"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Context windows","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/context-windows"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Token counting","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/token-counting"}],"text":"Models do not read characters or words. They read tokens, small pieces of text, and every limit and every price is counted in tokens. Knowing what a token is and what fills the context window explains why long chats get worse, why prices depend on length, and why a prompt that works on a short example can fail on real data. What you will learn What a token is, and rough sizes What counts toward the context window Input limits versus output limits Strategies for text that does not fit Tokens A tokenizer splits text into pieces such as whole common words, parts of rare words, punctuation and spaces. As a rule of thumb, one token is about four characters of English text, or three quarters of a word. Code, other languages and unusual strings often need more tokens per character. Different model families use different tokenizers, so counts differ. Use the provider&#39;s token counting tool rather than guessing when limits matter. The context window The context window is the total number of tokens the model can consider in one request: the system prompt, the conversation so far, any documents or tool results you include, and the answer being generated. When the sum is too large, the request fails or older text must be dropped. Long contexts also cost more and can reduce accuracy for details buried in the middle, so more text is not always better. Input and output limits Providers set a maximum number of output tokens per reply, separate from the context size. If you ask for a long report with a small output limit, the answer is cut off, and the response says it stopped because of length. Always check the stop reason in the response. A truncated JSON document is invalid JSON, which breaks the program that parses it. response = client.messages.create( model=MODEL, max_tokens= 1024 , messages=[{ \"role\" : \"user\" , \"content\" : prompt}], ) if response.stop_reason == \"max_tokens\" : raise RuntimeError( \"answer was cut off; raise max_tokens or shorten the task\" ) When text does not fit Select : send only relevant parts, found by search or by simple rules. Summarise : compress old conversation turns into a short summary. Chunk : process a long document in pieces and combine the results. Cache : reuse a large, unchanging prefix, such as instructions or a reference document, using prompt caching to cut cost and delay. Measure : log input and output token counts for each request, so cost and growth are visible. Predict first. A chat application sends the full history every turn. After 40 turns the answers get slower and costlier. Why, and what do you change? Your guess Answer: Every turn resends all earlier tokens, so input grows each time. Summarise or trim old turns and cache stable parts. Input tokens add up. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Tokens, context windows and limits fits together Flow in order: 1, Tokens to The context window; 2, The context window to Input and output limits; 3, Input and output limits to When text does not fit. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Tokens Step What it does: A tokenizer splits text into pieces such as whole common words, parts of rare words, punctuation and spaces. If it fails or is weak: Guessing token counts instead of measuring. The context window Step What it does: The context window is the total number of tokens the model can consider in one request: the system prompt, the conversation so far, any documents or tool results you... If it fails or is weak: Putting entire documents in when a few paragraphs would do. Input and output limits Step What it does: Providers set a maximum number of output tokens per reply, separate from the context size. If it fails or is weak: Ignoring the stop reason. When text does not fit Step What it does: Select: send only r"},"llm/sampling-temperature-and-determinism":{"html":"\u003cp>After the model scores every possible next token, something must choose one. That choice is called sampling, and it explains why answers vary between runs. Getting predictable behaviour for a pipeline is partly a matter of settings, and mostly a matter of designing the system so that small variation does not matter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How sampling chooses a token\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What temperature and top-p do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What you can and cannot make deterministic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Designing around variation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"probabilities\">Probabilities\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each step the model produces a probability for every token. If &quot;Paris&quot; has 92 percent and &quot;Lyon&quot; has 3 percent after &quot;The capital of France is&quot;, then most runs choose Paris. Different prompts and different points in a sentence have very different spreads: a factual answer is peaked, a story opening is wide.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"temperature-and-top-p\">Temperature and top-p\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Temperature\u003c/strong> rescales probabilities before choosing. Low values (near 0) make the highest-probability token win almost always, giving focused and repeatable output. Higher values flatten the choice and give more varied, creative output. \u003cstrong>Top-p\u003c/strong> limits the choice to the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p. Change one of them, not both, unless you have a reason. For extraction, classification and code, use low temperature; for brainstorming, use higher.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"response = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=300,\n    temperature=0.0,\n    messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: &quot;Classify this ticket: &quot; + ticket}],\n)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">response = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">300\u003c/span>,\n    temperature=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.0\u003c/span>,\n    messages=[{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;role&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;content&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Classify this ticket: &quot;\u003c/span> + ticket}],\n)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"determinism-has-limits\">Determinism has limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Even at temperature 0, results can differ slightly between calls, because of how requests are batched and how hardware does arithmetic, and any change to the model version changes outputs. Some newer models and modes fix or ignore sampling settings, so check the documentation for the model you use. Never write tests that require an exact sentence from a model, unless you have pinned the model and accept occasional breaks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"designing-around-variation\">Designing around variation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ask for \u003cstrong>structured output\u003c/strong> and validate it; the format stays stable even when wording varies.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test \u003cstrong>properties\u003c/strong>, not strings: the answer must contain a valid category, a number in range, or cite a given document.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run an important test several times and look at the \u003cstrong>pass rate\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For decisions, use a constrained set of answers and a fallback to a human when the model is unsure.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pin a \u003cstrong>model version\u003c/strong> in production and re-run your tests before upgrading.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A unit test compares a model reply to an exact sentence and fails once a week. What should it check instead?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A property of the answer, such as valid JSON with an allowed category, run several times with a pass-rate threshold.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Test behaviour, not wording.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Sampling, temperature and determinism fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Probabilities\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Temperature and top-p\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Determinism has limits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Designing around variation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Probabilities to Temperature and top-p; 2, Temperature and top-p to Determinism has limits; 3, Determinism has limits to Designing around variation.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Probabilities \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> For each step the model produces a probability for every token.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not pinning the model version.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Temperature and top-p \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Temperature rescales probabilities before choosing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using high temperature for extraction or classification.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Determinism has limits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Even at temperature 0, results can differ slightly between calls, because of how requests are batched and how hardware does arithmetic, and any change to the model...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Expecting exact repeatability.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Designing around variation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ask for structured output and validate it; the format stays stable even when wording varies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-flaky-classifier\">Incident: The flaky classifier\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A ticket router sent the same message to different queues on different days.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do python route.py &quot;refund not received&quot;; done\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> 1 2 3 4 5; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span> python route.py \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;refund not received&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">billing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">billing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">billing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payments\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The prompt asked for free text, the temperature was left at the default, and &quot;payments&quot; and &quot;billing&quot; both seemed reasonable. The team listed the allowed queues in the prompt, requested one value from that list in JSON, set a low temperature, and added a test that runs each sample ten times.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using high temperature for extraction or classification\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting exact repeatability\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tuning temperature and top-p together without measuring\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not pinning the model version\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"probabilities","text":"Probabilities"},{"id":"temperature-and-top-p","text":"Temperature and top-p"},{"id":"determinism-has-limits","text":"Determinism has limits"},{"id":"designing-around-variation","text":"Designing around variation"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-flaky-classifier","text":"Incident: The flaky classifier"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic API reference: Messages","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/api/messages"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Reduce hallucinations","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations"}],"text":"After the model scores every possible next token, something must choose one. That choice is called sampling, and it explains why answers vary between runs. Getting predictable behaviour for a pipeline is partly a matter of settings, and mostly a matter of designing the system so that small variation does not matter. What you will learn How sampling chooses a token What temperature and top-p do What you can and cannot make deterministic Designing around variation Probabilities For each step the model produces a probability for every token. If \"Paris\" has 92 percent and \"Lyon\" has 3 percent after \"The capital of France is\", then most runs choose Paris. Different prompts and different points in a sentence have very different spreads: a factual answer is peaked, a story opening is wide. Temperature and top-p Temperature rescales probabilities before choosing. Low values (near 0) make the highest-probability token win almost always, giving focused and repeatable output. Higher values flatten the choice and give more varied, creative output. Top-p limits the choice to the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p. Change one of them, not both, unless you have a reason. For extraction, classification and code, use low temperature; for brainstorming, use higher. response = client.messages.create( model=MODEL, max_tokens= 300 , temperature= 0.0 , messages=[{ \"role\" : \"user\" , \"content\" : \"Classify this ticket: \" + ticket}], ) Determinism has limits Even at temperature 0, results can differ slightly between calls, because of how requests are batched and how hardware does arithmetic, and any change to the model version changes outputs. Some newer models and modes fix or ignore sampling settings, so check the documentation for the model you use. Never write tests that require an exact sentence from a model, unless you have pinned the model and accept occasional breaks. Designing around variation Ask for structured output and validate it; the format stays stable even when wording varies. Test properties , not strings: the answer must contain a valid category, a number in range, or cite a given document. Run an important test several times and look at the pass rate . For decisions, use a constrained set of answers and a fallback to a human when the model is unsure. Pin a model version in production and re-run your tests before upgrading. Predict first. A unit test compares a model reply to an exact sentence and fails once a week. What should it check instead? Your guess Answer: A property of the answer, such as valid JSON with an allowed category, run several times with a pass-rate threshold. Test behaviour, not wording. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Sampling, temperature and determinism fits together Flow in order: 1, Probabilities to Temperature and top-p; 2, Temperature and top-p to Determinism has limits; 3, Determinism has limits to Designing around variation. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Probabilities Step What it does: For each step the model produces a probability for every token. If it fails or is weak: Not pinning the model version. Temperature and top-p Step What it does: Temperature rescales probabilities before choosing. If it fails or is weak: Using high temperature for extraction or classification. Determinism has limits Step What it does: Even at temperature 0, results can differ slightly between calls, because of how requests are batched and how hardware does arithmetic, and any change to the model... If it fails or is weak: Expecting exact repeatability. Designing around variation Step What it does: Ask for structured output and validate it; the format stays stable even when wording varies. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this d"},"llm/training-fine-tuning-and-where-knowledge-comes-from":{"html":"\u003cp>A model&#39;s knowledge comes from training, and training ended at some date. Your documents, your tickets and yesterday&#39;s news were never part of it. Understanding the stages of training, and the difference between knowledge in the model and knowledge in the prompt, tells you when to use context, retrieval, tools or training.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Pre-training and post-training in plain terms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The knowledge cutoff and why facts go stale\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why hallucinations happen\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ways to add knowledge: context, retrieval, tools, fine-tuning\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stages-of-training\">Stages of training\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Pre-training\u003c/strong> teaches the model to predict text from a huge collection of documents and code. This gives it language, broad facts and skills. \u003cstrong>Post-training\u003c/strong> then shapes behaviour with examples and feedback so that the model follows instructions, refuses harmful requests and writes in a helpful style. Together they produce an assistant. The details differ by provider, and are not something you control when you call an API.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cutoff\">The cutoff\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The training data ends at a date, called the knowledge cutoff. After that, the model knows nothing unless you provide it. This affects library versions, product names, prices, laws and current events. A model may also be unsure about its own cutoff, so do not ask it what is current. Put the current date and the facts you need into the prompt, or give it a search tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-it-makes-things-up\">Why it makes things up\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The model is trained to produce plausible text, and when the answer is not in its training or in your prompt, a plausible-sounding answer is still the most likely continuation. Rare facts, precise numbers, citations and names of obscure APIs are the most common places it invents. You reduce this by giving source text and asking for answers only from it, allowing &quot;I don&#39;t know&quot; as a valid answer, and asking for quotes that you can check.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"adding-knowledge\">Adding knowledge\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Put it in the prompt\u003c/strong> (context): best for small, current, private facts.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Retrieve it\u003c/strong>: search your documents and include the best passages (RAG).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Give tools\u003c/strong>: let the model call a database, an API or a search and use the result.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Fine-tune\u003c/strong>: train further on your examples. Useful for style, format or narrow tasks at scale; poor at adding frequently changing facts, and it costs effort to evaluate and maintain.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Start at the top of this list. Most applications never need the last one.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A support bot must answer using last week's policy changes. Which approach fits, and why not fine-tuning?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Retrieve the current policy text and put it in the prompt. Fine-tuning is slow to update and poor for facts that change often.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Facts change; keep them in context.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Training, knowledge and hallucination fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Stages of training\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The cutoff\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Why it makes things up\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Adding knowledge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Stages of training to The cutoff; 2, The cutoff to Why it makes things up; 3, Why it makes things up to Adding knowledge.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Stages of training \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pre-training teaches the model to predict text from a huge collection of documents and code.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assuming the model knows recent or private facts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>The cutoff \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The training data ends at a date, called the knowledge cutoff.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fine-tuning to add facts that change.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Why it makes things up \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The model is trained to produce plausible text, and when the answer is not in its training or in your prompt, a plausible-sounding answer is still the most likely...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not allowing the model to say it does not know.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Adding knowledge \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Put it in the prompt (context): best for small, current, private facts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Trusting citations without opening them.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-library-that-did-not-exist\">Incident: The library that did not exist\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A model suggested a function that failed on first use.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -c &quot;import boto3; boto3.client('s3').upload_stream('a', 'b')&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;import boto3; boto3.client(&#x27;s3&#x27;).upload_stream(&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;b&#x27;)&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">AttributeError: 'S3' object has no attribute 'upload_stream'\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The model blended real method names into one that sounded right. The developer checked the official reference and found the correct call. The team now pastes the relevant documentation page into the prompt for unfamiliar APIs and reviews generated code with tests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming the model knows recent or private facts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fine-tuning to add facts that change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not allowing the model to say it does not know\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting citations without opening them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"stages-of-training","text":"Stages of training"},{"id":"the-cutoff","text":"The cutoff"},{"id":"why-it-makes-things-up","text":"Why it makes things up"},{"id":"adding-knowledge","text":"Adding knowledge"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-library-that-did-not-exist","text":"Incident: The library that did not exist"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Reduce hallucinations","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Models overview","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/about-claude/models/overview"}],"text":"A model&#39;s knowledge comes from training, and training ended at some date. Your documents, your tickets and yesterday&#39;s news were never part of it. Understanding the stages of training, and the difference between knowledge in the model and knowledge in the prompt, tells you when to use context, retrieval, tools or training. What you will learn Pre-training and post-training in plain terms The knowledge cutoff and why facts go stale Why hallucinations happen Ways to add knowledge: context, retrieval, tools, fine-tuning Stages of training Pre-training teaches the model to predict text from a huge collection of documents and code. This gives it language, broad facts and skills. Post-training then shapes behaviour with examples and feedback so that the model follows instructions, refuses harmful requests and writes in a helpful style. Together they produce an assistant. The details differ by provider, and are not something you control when you call an API. The cutoff The training data ends at a date, called the knowledge cutoff. After that, the model knows nothing unless you provide it. This affects library versions, product names, prices, laws and current events. A model may also be unsure about its own cutoff, so do not ask it what is current. Put the current date and the facts you need into the prompt, or give it a search tool. Why it makes things up The model is trained to produce plausible text, and when the answer is not in its training or in your prompt, a plausible-sounding answer is still the most likely continuation. Rare facts, precise numbers, citations and names of obscure APIs are the most common places it invents. You reduce this by giving source text and asking for answers only from it, allowing \"I don&#39;t know\" as a valid answer, and asking for quotes that you can check. Adding knowledge Put it in the prompt (context): best for small, current, private facts. Retrieve it : search your documents and include the best passages (RAG). Give tools : let the model call a database, an API or a search and use the result. Fine-tune : train further on your examples. Useful for style, format or narrow tasks at scale; poor at adding frequently changing facts, and it costs effort to evaluate and maintain. Start at the top of this list. Most applications never need the last one. Predict first. A support bot must answer using last week's policy changes. Which approach fits, and why not fine-tuning? Your guess Answer: Retrieve the current policy text and put it in the prompt. Fine-tuning is slow to update and poor for facts that change often. Facts change; keep them in context. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Training, knowledge and hallucination fits together Flow in order: 1, Stages of training to The cutoff; 2, The cutoff to Why it makes things up; 3, Why it makes things up to Adding knowledge. Step Network edge Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Stages of training Step What it does: Pre-training teaches the model to predict text from a huge collection of documents and code. If it fails or is weak: Assuming the model knows recent or private facts. The cutoff Step What it does: The training data ends at a date, called the knowledge cutoff. If it fails or is weak: Fine-tuning to add facts that change. Why it makes things up Step What it does: The model is trained to produce plausible text, and when the answer is not in its training or in your prompt, a plausible-sounding answer is still the most likely... If it fails or is weak: Not allowing the model to say it does not know. Adding knowledge Network edge What it does: Put it in the prompt (context): best for small, current, private facts. If it fails or is weak: Trusting citations without opening them. Incident: The library that d"},"llm/calling-an-llm-api":{"html":"\u003cp>Using a model from code is an ordinary HTTP API call with unusual inputs. The details that matter are the same as for any service: authentication, timeouts, retries, rate limits, logging and cost. Getting them right turns a demo into something that runs on a schedule at three in the morning.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The shape of a request: model, messages, limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roles: system, user, assistant\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Streaming and timeouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Errors, rate limits, retries and key handling\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-first-call\">A first call\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import os\nimport anthropic\n\nMODEL = os.environ[&quot;MODEL_ID&quot;]   # set the model name in configuration, not in code\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()    # reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from the environment\n\nresponse = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=500,\n    system=&quot;You are a concise assistant for site reliability engineers.&quot;,\n    messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: &quot;Explain a 502 error in two sentences.&quot;}],\n)\nprint(response.content[0].text)\nprint(response.usage.input_tokens, response.usage.output_tokens)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> os\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> anthropic\n\nMODEL = os.environ[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;MODEL_ID&quot;\u003c/span>]   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># set the model name in configuration, not in code\u003c/span>\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from the environment\u003c/span>\n\nresponse = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">500\u003c/span>,\n    system=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;You are a concise assistant for site reliability engineers.&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    messages=[{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;role&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;content&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Explain a 502 error in two sentences.&quot;\u003c/span>}],\n)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(response.content[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>].text)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(response.usage.input_tokens, response.usage.output_tokens)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A request names a model, a limit on output tokens, an optional system prompt and a list of messages. The reply has content blocks, a stop reason and token usage. Other providers have similar shapes with different field names, so wrap the call in your own small function.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles-and-conversation-state\">Roles and conversation state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Messages alternate between the \u003cstrong>user\u003c/strong> and the \u003cstrong>assistant\u003c/strong>. The \u003cstrong>system\u003c/strong> prompt sets rules and context for the whole conversation. The API is stateless: it remembers nothing between calls, so your program sends the whole history each time and decides what to keep, trim or summarise. Store conversations in your own database, and treat them as personal data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"streaming-and-timeouts\">Streaming and timeouts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Long answers take seconds. Streaming returns tokens as they are generated, so users see text immediately and your server can stop early. Always set a client timeout, and handle a stream that ends in the middle.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"with client.messages.stream(model=MODEL, max_tokens=500, messages=messages) as stream:\n    for text in stream.text_stream:\n        print(text, end=&quot;&quot;, flush=True)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> client.messages.stream(model=MODEL, max_tokens=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">500\u003c/span>, messages=messages) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> stream:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> text \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> stream.text_stream:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(text, end=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>, flush=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"errors-limits-and-keys\">Errors, limits and keys\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Rate limits\u003c/strong> return a 429 status: slow down, and retry with exponential backoff and jitter. Respect any retry-after header.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Server errors\u003c/strong> (500, 529 overloaded) are usually temporary: retry a few times, then fail clearly.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Client errors\u003c/strong> (400, 401, 403) will not fix themselves: do not retry; log the message.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Keys\u003c/strong> belong in a secret manager or environment variable, never in code, logs, client-side JavaScript or screenshots. Use separate keys per service and per environment, set spend limits, and rotate on suspicion.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Log\u003c/strong> request IDs, model, token counts, latency and outcome, but not secrets or personal data.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your batch job gets 429 errors during a large run. What do you add?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Backoff with jitter, a limit on concurrency, and respect for retry-after; consider batching or a queue.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Slow down instead of hammering.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Calling an LLM API fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A first call\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Roles and conversation state\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Streaming and timeouts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Errors, limits and keys\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, A first call to Roles and conversation state; 2, Roles and conversation state to Streaming and timeouts; 3, Streaming and timeouts to Errors, limits and keys.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>A first call \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A request names a model, a limit on output tokens, an optional system prompt and a list of messages.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retrying client errors.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Roles and conversation state \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Messages alternate between the user and the assistant.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Logging full prompts that contain personal data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Streaming and timeouts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Long answers take seconds. Streaming returns tokens as they are generated, so users see text immediately and your server can stop early.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No timeout on API calls.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Errors, limits and keys \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rate limits return a 429 status: slow down, and retry with exponential backoff and jitter.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hard-coding keys.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-key-in-the-screenshot\">Incident: The key in the screenshot\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A teammate shared a terminal screenshot in a public channel that included an API key.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"env | grep -i anthropic\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code}\\n&quot; https://api.example.com/v1/models -H &quot;x-api-key: $OLD_KEY&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span> | grep -i anthropic\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%{http_code}\\n&quot;\u003c/span> https://api.example.com/v1/models -H \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;x-api-key: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$OLD_KEY\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">401\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The key was revoked as soon as the message was noticed, which is why the check now returns 401. A new key went into the secret manager, usage logs were reviewed for unexpected requests, and the team stopped printing environment variables in shared screens and added secret scanning to chat and repositories.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Hard-coding keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying client errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeout on API calls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging full prompts that contain personal data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-first-call","text":"A first call"},{"id":"roles-and-conversation-state","text":"Roles and conversation state"},{"id":"streaming-and-timeouts","text":"Streaming and timeouts"},{"id":"errors-limits-and-keys","text":"Errors, limits and keys"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-key-in-the-screenshot","text":"Incident: The key in the screenshot"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic API reference: Messages","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/api/messages"},{"t":"Anthropic API: Rate limits","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/api/rate-limits"}],"text":"Using a model from code is an ordinary HTTP API call with unusual inputs. The details that matter are the same as for any service: authentication, timeouts, retries, rate limits, logging and cost. Getting them right turns a demo into something that runs on a schedule at three in the morning. What you will learn The shape of a request: model, messages, limits Roles: system, user, assistant Streaming and timeouts Errors, rate limits, retries and key handling A first call import os import anthropic MODEL = os.environ[ \"MODEL_ID\" ] # set the model name in configuration, not in code client = anthropic.Anthropic() # reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from the environment response = client.messages.create( model=MODEL, max_tokens= 500 , system= \"You are a concise assistant for site reliability engineers.\" , messages=[{ \"role\" : \"user\" , \"content\" : \"Explain a 502 error in two sentences.\" }], ) print (response.content[ 0 ].text) print (response.usage.input_tokens, response.usage.output_tokens) A request names a model, a limit on output tokens, an optional system prompt and a list of messages. The reply has content blocks, a stop reason and token usage. Other providers have similar shapes with different field names, so wrap the call in your own small function. Roles and conversation state Messages alternate between the user and the assistant . The system prompt sets rules and context for the whole conversation. The API is stateless: it remembers nothing between calls, so your program sends the whole history each time and decides what to keep, trim or summarise. Store conversations in your own database, and treat them as personal data. Streaming and timeouts Long answers take seconds. Streaming returns tokens as they are generated, so users see text immediately and your server can stop early. Always set a client timeout, and handle a stream that ends in the middle. with client.messages.stream(model=MODEL, max_tokens= 500 , messages=messages) as stream: for text in stream.text_stream: print (text, end= \"\" , flush= True ) Errors, limits and keys Rate limits return a 429 status: slow down, and retry with exponential backoff and jitter. Respect any retry-after header. Server errors (500, 529 overloaded) are usually temporary: retry a few times, then fail clearly. Client errors (400, 401, 403) will not fix themselves: do not retry; log the message. Keys belong in a secret manager or environment variable, never in code, logs, client-side JavaScript or screenshots. Use separate keys per service and per environment, set spend limits, and rotate on suspicion. Log request IDs, model, token counts, latency and outcome, but not secrets or personal data. Predict first. Your batch job gets 429 errors during a large run. What do you add? Your guess Answer: Backoff with jitter, a limit on concurrency, and respect for retry-after; consider batching or a queue. Slow down instead of hammering. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Calling an LLM API fits together Flow in order: 1, A first call to Roles and conversation state; 2, Roles and conversation state to Streaming and timeouts; 3, Streaming and timeouts to Errors, limits and keys. Step Data store Queue Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. A first call Step What it does: A request names a model, a limit on output tokens, an optional system prompt and a list of messages. If it fails or is weak: Retrying client errors. Roles and conversation state Data store What it does: Messages alternate between the user and the assistant. If it fails or is weak: Logging full prompts that contain personal data. Streaming and timeouts Queue What it does: Long answers take seconds. Streaming returns tokens as they are generated, so users see text immediately and your server can stop early. If it "},"llm/cost-latency-and-caching":{"html":"\u003cp>Model calls are priced per token and take noticeable time. A feature that is cheap in a demo can be expensive at ten thousand users a day, and slow enough to frustrate people. A little arithmetic before launch, and a few well-known techniques, keep both under control.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Estimate cost from tokens and traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Time to first token and tokens per second\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cut cost: smaller models, shorter prompts, caching, batching\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Budgets and alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"back-of-envelope-cost\">Back-of-envelope cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cost per request = input tokens times the input price, plus output tokens times the output price. Output tokens usually cost several times more than input tokens. Example with made-up prices: 3,000 input tokens at 3 dollars per million is 0.009 dollars, 500 output tokens at 15 dollars per million is 0.0075 dollars, so about 1.65 cents per request. At 50,000 requests per day that is about 825 dollars a day. Use your provider&#39;s current price list, and measure your real token counts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def cost(inp, out, price_in=3.0, price_out=15.0):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Prices are dollars per million tokens (example values).&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    return inp / 1e6 * price_in + out / 1e6 * price_out\n\nprint(round(cost(3000, 500) * 50_000, 2))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">cost\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">inp, out, price_in=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3.0\u003c/span>, price_out=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">15.0\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;&quot;Prices are dollars per million tokens (example values).&quot;&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> inp / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1e6\u003c/span> * price_in + out / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1e6\u003c/span> * price_out\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(cost(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3000\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">500\u003c/span>) * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">50_000\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"latency\">Latency\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Total time is roughly: network and queueing, \u003cstrong>time to first token\u003c/strong> (processing your input), and then output length divided by \u003cstrong>tokens per second\u003c/strong>. A long prompt delays the first token; a long answer takes long to finish. Streaming improves perceived speed. Smaller models are faster and cheaper, so use the smallest model that passes your tests for each step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reducing-cost-and-delay\">Reducing cost and delay\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Right-size the model\u003c/strong>: easy steps (classify, extract) on a small model, hard steps on a large one.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Shorten\u003c/strong>: trim instructions, remove unused context, ask for concise output or a fixed format.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prompt caching\u003c/strong>: mark a large stable prefix (instructions, reference text, tool definitions) so repeated requests reuse it at lower cost and delay. Put stable content first and changing content last.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Batch\u003c/strong> non-urgent work through a batch interface, often at a discount.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cache answers\u003c/strong> to identical questions in your own store, if the content allows.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Limit\u003c/strong> output with max_tokens and stop conditions.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"budgets-and-visibility\">Budgets and visibility\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Track tokens and cost per feature, per customer and per model. Set spend limits at the provider and alerts in your monitoring. Add a circuit breaker for runaway loops, especially in agents that call a model repeatedly. One forgotten retry loop can spend a month&#39;s budget overnight.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A feature calls the large model three times per request to classify, extract and summarise. How can you cut cost without losing quality?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Use a small model for classification and extraction, cache the stable prefix, and keep the large model for the summary only; confirm with your tests.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Match model size to the step.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Cost, latency and caching fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Back-of-envelope cost\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Latency\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reducing cost and delay\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Budgets and visibility\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Back-of-envelope cost to Latency; 2, Latency to Reducing cost and delay; 3, Reducing cost and delay to Budgets and visibility.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Back-of-envelope cost \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cost per request = input tokens times the input price, plus output tokens times the output price.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No token or cost tracking.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Latency \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Total time is roughly: network and queueing, time to first token (processing your input), and then output length divided by tokens per second.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using the largest model for every step.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Reducing cost and delay \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Right-size the model: easy steps (classify, extract) on a small model, hard steps on a large one.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Putting changing text before the stable prefix.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Budgets and visibility \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Track tokens and cost per feature, per customer and per model.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No budget limit on automated loops.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-overnight-bill\">Incident: The overnight bill\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A scheduled job re-sent the same 40,000-token document 2,000 times.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python usage_report.py --day yesterday\npython usage_report.py --day yesterday --cached\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python usage_report.py --day yesterday\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">feature=doc-summary requests=2000 input_tokens=80,412,000 output_tokens=610,000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python usage_report.py --day yesterday --cached\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cache_read_tokens=0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Nothing was cached, and the document was the same in every request. The team moved the document into a cached prefix, used a batch process for the nightly run, and added a daily spend alert and a hard stop at a set budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No token or cost tracking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using the largest model for every step\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting changing text before the stable prefix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No budget limit on automated loops\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"back-of-envelope-cost","text":"Back-of-envelope cost"},{"id":"latency","text":"Latency"},{"id":"reducing-cost-and-delay","text":"Reducing cost and delay"},{"id":"budgets-and-visibility","text":"Budgets and visibility"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-overnight-bill","text":"Incident: The overnight bill"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Prompt caching","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Message Batches","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/batch-processing"}],"text":"Model calls are priced per token and take noticeable time. A feature that is cheap in a demo can be expensive at ten thousand users a day, and slow enough to frustrate people. A little arithmetic before launch, and a few well-known techniques, keep both under control. What you will learn Estimate cost from tokens and traffic Time to first token and tokens per second Cut cost: smaller models, shorter prompts, caching, batching Budgets and alerts Back-of-envelope cost Cost per request = input tokens times the input price, plus output tokens times the output price. Output tokens usually cost several times more than input tokens. Example with made-up prices: 3,000 input tokens at 3 dollars per million is 0.009 dollars, 500 output tokens at 15 dollars per million is 0.0075 dollars, so about 1.65 cents per request. At 50,000 requests per day that is about 825 dollars a day. Use your provider&#39;s current price list, and measure your real token counts. def cost ( inp, out, price_in= 3.0 , price_out= 15.0 ): \"\"\"Prices are dollars per million tokens (example values).\"\"\" return inp / 1e6 * price_in + out / 1e6 * price_out print ( round (cost( 3000 , 500 ) * 50_000 , 2 )) Latency Total time is roughly: network and queueing, time to first token (processing your input), and then output length divided by tokens per second . A long prompt delays the first token; a long answer takes long to finish. Streaming improves perceived speed. Smaller models are faster and cheaper, so use the smallest model that passes your tests for each step. Reducing cost and delay Right-size the model : easy steps (classify, extract) on a small model, hard steps on a large one. Shorten : trim instructions, remove unused context, ask for concise output or a fixed format. Prompt caching : mark a large stable prefix (instructions, reference text, tool definitions) so repeated requests reuse it at lower cost and delay. Put stable content first and changing content last. Batch non-urgent work through a batch interface, often at a discount. Cache answers to identical questions in your own store, if the content allows. Limit output with max_tokens and stop conditions. Budgets and visibility Track tokens and cost per feature, per customer and per model. Set spend limits at the provider and alerts in your monitoring. Add a circuit breaker for runaway loops, especially in agents that call a model repeatedly. One forgotten retry loop can spend a month&#39;s budget overnight. Predict first. A feature calls the large model three times per request to classify, extract and summarise. How can you cut cost without losing quality? Your guess Answer: Use a small model for classification and extraction, cache the stable prefix, and keep the large model for the summary only; confirm with your tests. Match model size to the step. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Cost, latency and caching fits together Flow in order: 1, Back-of-envelope cost to Latency; 2, Latency to Reducing cost and delay; 3, Reducing cost and delay to Budgets and visibility. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Back-of-envelope cost Step What it does: Cost per request = input tokens times the input price, plus output tokens times the output price. If it fails or is weak: No token or cost tracking. Latency Step What it does: Total time is roughly: network and queueing, time to first token (processing your input), and then output length divided by tokens per second. If it fails or is weak: Using the largest model for every step. Reducing cost and delay Step What it does: Right-size the model: easy steps (classify, extract) on a small model, hard steps on a large one. If it fails or is weak: Putting changing text before the stable prefix. Budgets and visibility Step What it doe"},"llm/choosing-and-evaluating-models":{"html":"\u003cp>Public benchmarks measure general ability. Your feature needs one specific thing done well on your data. The only reliable way to choose a model or a prompt is to test it on examples that look like your real traffic, and to keep those tests running as things change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why you need your own evaluation set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build a set of examples with expected properties\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Score outputs: exact checks, rules and model-based grading\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compare, decide and keep testing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-your-own-tests\">Why your own tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Benchmarks can be outdated, narrow, or leaked into training data, and they use tasks unlike yours. A model that ranks first on a leaderboard may do worse on your support tickets or your log format. A small set of 30 to 100 real, varied examples with known good answers will tell you more about your feature than any public score.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-the-set\">Build the set\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Collect real inputs (with personal data removed), include normal cases, hard cases, and cases where the right answer is &quot;I don&#39;t know&quot; or &quot;escalate&quot;. For each, write what a good answer must contain or must not contain. Keep a held-out part you do not tune on. Store the set in version control next to the prompt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{&quot;id&quot;: &quot;t-014&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;Customer says card was charged twice&quot;, &quot;expect&quot;: {&quot;category&quot;: &quot;billing&quot;, &quot;must_not_say&quot;: [&quot;refund approved&quot;]}}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;id&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;t-014&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;input&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Customer says card was charged twice&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;expect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;category&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;billing&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;must_not_say&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;refund approved&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"score-the-answers\">Score the answers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Exact checks\u003c/strong> for structured fields: category in the allowed list, number in range, valid JSON.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Rules\u003c/strong> for text: contains required facts, avoids forbidden claims, stays under a length.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Model-based grading\u003c/strong>: another model compares the answer to a rubric. Fast and useful, but it makes mistakes and has biases, so spot-check it with humans and prefer clear rubrics with yes or no questions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Human review\u003c/strong> of a sample, especially for tone and safety.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def score(case, output):\n    ok = output.get(&quot;category&quot;) in ALLOWED\n    ok = ok and output.get(&quot;category&quot;) == case[&quot;expect&quot;][&quot;category&quot;]\n    ok = ok and not any(w in output.get(&quot;reply&quot;, &quot;&quot;) for w in case[&quot;expect&quot;].get(&quot;must_not_say&quot;, []))\n    return ok\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">score\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">case\u003c/span>, output\u003c/span>):\n    ok = output.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;category&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> ALLOWED\n    ok = ok \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> output.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;category&quot;\u003c/span>) == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">case\u003c/span>[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expect&quot;\u003c/span>][\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;category&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    ok = ok \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">any\u003c/span>(w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> output.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;reply&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">case\u003c/span>[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expect&quot;\u003c/span>].get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;must_not_say&quot;\u003c/span>, []))\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> ok\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"decide-and-keep-testing\">Decide and keep testing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run each candidate model and prompt on the same set, several times, and compare pass rate, cost and latency. Choose the cheapest option that meets your quality bar. Re-run the set when you change the prompt, the model version, the retrieval, or the tools. Add every production failure as a new test case. This is a regression test for behaviour that cannot be unit tested in the old way.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A new model version scores higher on a public benchmark. Should you switch production to it today?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Not before running your own evaluation set; check quality, cost and latency on your data, then roll out gradually.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Your data decides.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Choosing and evaluating models fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Why your own tests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Build the set\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Score the answers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Decide and keep testing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Why your own tests to Build the set; 2, Build the set to Score the answers; 3, Score the answers to Decide and keep testing.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Why your own tests \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Benchmarks can be outdated, narrow, or leaked into training data, and they use tasks unlike yours.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Choosing a model by leaderboard alone.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Build the set \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Collect real inputs (with personal data removed), include normal cases, hard cases, and cases where the right answer is &quot;I don't know&quot; or &quot;escalate&quot;.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using only model-based grading with no human check.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Score the answers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Exact checks for structured fields: category in the allowed list, number in range, valid JSON.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Decide and keep testing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run each candidate model and prompt on the same set, several times, and compare pass rate, cost and latency.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Tuning on the same examples you evaluate on.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-upgrade-that-broke-the-router\">Incident: The upgrade that broke the router\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A model upgrade changed how the ticket router phrased its categories.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python run_evals.py --model new --set tickets\npython run_evals.py --model old --set tickets\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python run_evals.py --model new --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> tickets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pass_rate=0.71 (was 0.94)   failures: category_not_in_allowed_list=38\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python run_evals.py --model old --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> tickets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pass_rate=0.94\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new version added explanations to the category field, so parsing failed. Because the team had an evaluation set in CI, the problem showed up before release. They tightened the prompt and the schema, re-ran the set until the pass rate was back, and then switched gradually.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing a model by leaderboard alone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tuning on the same examples you evaluate on\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using only model-based grading with no human check\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not re-running tests after changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-your-own-tests","text":"Why your own tests"},{"id":"build-the-set","text":"Build the set"},{"id":"score-the-answers","text":"Score the answers"},{"id":"decide-and-keep-testing","text":"Decide and keep testing"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-upgrade-that-broke-the-router","text":"Incident: The upgrade that broke the router"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Define success criteria","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/define-success"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Develop test cases","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests"}],"text":"Public benchmarks measure general ability. Your feature needs one specific thing done well on your data. The only reliable way to choose a model or a prompt is to test it on examples that look like your real traffic, and to keep those tests running as things change. What you will learn Why you need your own evaluation set Build a set of examples with expected properties Score outputs: exact checks, rules and model-based grading Compare, decide and keep testing Why your own tests Benchmarks can be outdated, narrow, or leaked into training data, and they use tasks unlike yours. A model that ranks first on a leaderboard may do worse on your support tickets or your log format. A small set of 30 to 100 real, varied examples with known good answers will tell you more about your feature than any public score. Build the set Collect real inputs (with personal data removed), include normal cases, hard cases, and cases where the right answer is \"I don&#39;t know\" or \"escalate\". For each, write what a good answer must contain or must not contain. Keep a held-out part you do not tune on. Store the set in version control next to the prompt. { \"id\" : \"t-014\" , \"input\" : \"Customer says card was charged twice\" , \"expect\" : { \"category\" : \"billing\" , \"must_not_say\" : [ \"refund approved\" ] } } Score the answers Exact checks for structured fields: category in the allowed list, number in range, valid JSON. Rules for text: contains required facts, avoids forbidden claims, stays under a length. Model-based grading : another model compares the answer to a rubric. Fast and useful, but it makes mistakes and has biases, so spot-check it with humans and prefer clear rubrics with yes or no questions. Human review of a sample, especially for tone and safety. def score ( case , output ): ok = output.get( \"category\" ) in ALLOWED ok = ok and output.get( \"category\" ) == case [ \"expect\" ][ \"category\" ] ok = ok and not any (w in output.get( \"reply\" , \"\" ) for w in case [ \"expect\" ].get( \"must_not_say\" , [])) return ok Decide and keep testing Run each candidate model and prompt on the same set, several times, and compare pass rate, cost and latency. Choose the cheapest option that meets your quality bar. Re-run the set when you change the prompt, the model version, the retrieval, or the tools. Add every production failure as a new test case. This is a regression test for behaviour that cannot be unit tested in the old way. Predict first. A new model version scores higher on a public benchmark. Should you switch production to it today? Your guess Answer: Not before running your own evaluation set; check quality, cost and latency on your data, then roll out gradually. Your data decides. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Choosing and evaluating models fits together Flow in order: 1, Why your own tests to Build the set; 2, Build the set to Score the answers; 3, Score the answers to Decide and keep testing. Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Why your own tests Observability What it does: Benchmarks can be outdated, narrow, or leaked into training data, and they use tasks unlike yours. If it fails or is weak: Choosing a model by leaderboard alone. Build the set Step What it does: Collect real inputs (with personal data removed), include normal cases, hard cases, and cases where the right answer is \"I don't know\" or \"escalate\". If it fails or is weak: Using only model-based grading with no human check. Score the answers Step What it does: Exact checks for structured fields: category in the allowed list, number in range, valid JSON. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Decide and keep testing Observability What it does: Run each candidate model"},"llm/debugging-llm-applications":{"html":"\u003cp>When an LLM feature misbehaves there is no stack trace. The system is probabilistic and the failure may be in retrieval, the prompt, the model, the limits or the code around it. A fixed order of checks finds the cause quickly and turns each incident into a permanent test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Capture the exact request and response\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check limits, stop reasons and errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separate retrieval, prompt, model and parsing problems\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reproduce, fix and add a regression test\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capture-everything\">Capture everything\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You cannot debug what you cannot see. For every request, log the request ID, model and version, the full assembled prompt (with personal data protected), parameters, retrieved passages, tool calls and results, the raw reply, the stop reason, token counts and latency. Store them for a limited time with access controls. A trace that connects one user report to one stored request is worth more than any guess.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"first-checks\">First checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"jq '{id, model, stop_reason, usage, error}' logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json\njq -r '.prompt' logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json | head -60\njq -r '.reply' logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{id, model, stop_reason, usage, error}&#x27;\u003c/span> logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json\njq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.prompt&#x27;\u003c/span> logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -60\njq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.reply&#x27;\u003c/span> logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Look for: an error or a \u003cstrong>429/529\u003c/strong>; \u003cstrong>stop_reason\u003c/strong> other than a normal end (cut off by length); the \u003cstrong>model version\u003c/strong> changed; the prompt contains stale or missing text; a very large input that pushed out instructions; the reply that is valid text but fails your parser.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"which-layer-failed\">Which layer failed?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Retrieval or context\u003c/strong>: is the needed fact in the prompt? If not, fix search, chunking or the data. The model cannot use what it never saw.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prompt\u003c/strong>: is the instruction clear, are examples consistent, do two rules conflict? Try the same input with a simplified prompt.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Model\u003c/strong>: with the right facts and a clear prompt, does it still fail? Try a stronger model or break the task into steps.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Parsing and code\u003c/strong>: is the output valid but your code mishandles it, for example by assuming the first content block is text?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Environment\u003c/strong>: did a tool fail silently, or return an error that the model had to guess around?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fix-and-lock-it-in\">Fix and lock it in\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Reproduce with the stored request, change one thing, and re-run. Then add the case to the evaluation set with the expected behaviour, so it cannot return unnoticed. Compare the new pass rate with the old on the whole set, not only on the case you fixed. Record the cause in your runbook: most incidents repeat. Add monitors for the signals that would have caught this earlier, such as parse-failure rate, empty-answer rate, refusal rate, token growth and user thumbs-down rate.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A support bot suddenly says &quot;I don't have that information&quot; for questions it answered yesterday. Which layer do you check first?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Retrieval and context: check whether the relevant document is still being found and included, before blaming the model.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Is the fact in the prompt?\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Debugging LLM applications fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Capture everything\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">First checks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Which layer failed?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix and lock it in\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Capture everything to First checks; 2, First checks to Which layer failed?; 3, Which layer failed? to Fix and lock it in.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Capture everything \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> You cannot debug what you cannot see. For every request, log the request ID, model and version, the full assembled prompt (with personal data protected), parameters...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Blaming the model before checking the prompt contents.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>First checks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Look for: an error or a 429/529; stop_reason other than a normal end (cut off by length); the model version changed; the prompt contains stale or missing text; a very...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not logging the assembled prompt.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Which layer failed? \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Retrieval or context: is the needed fact in the prompt?\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The model cannot use what it never saw.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Fix and lock it in \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reproduce with the stored request, change one thing, and re-run.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fixing one case without a regression test.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-bot-that-stopped-finding-the-policy\">Incident: The bot that stopped finding the policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a documentation update, a support assistant began refusing policy questions.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"jq '{retrieved: [.retrieved[].source], stop_reason}' req-1204.json\njq '.retrieved[].source' req-0711-last-week.json\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{retrieved: [.retrieved[].source], stop_reason}&#x27;\u003c/span> req-1204.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{ &quot;retrieved&quot;: [&quot;blog/holiday-hours.md&quot;, &quot;faq/shipping.md&quot;], &quot;stop_reason&quot;: &quot;end_turn&quot; }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.retrieved[].source&#x27;\u003c/span> req-0711-last-week.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;policies/returns-v3.md&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The reindexing job had skipped the policies folder after a path change, so the returns policy was never retrieved and the model correctly said it did not know. The index was rebuilt, a monitor was added for documents that are not found for known test questions, and those questions became part of the nightly evaluation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Blaming the model before checking the prompt contents\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not logging the assembled prompt\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing one case without a regression test\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing several things at once\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"capture-everything","text":"Capture everything"},{"id":"first-checks","text":"First checks"},{"id":"which-layer-failed","text":"Which layer failed?"},{"id":"fix-and-lock-it-in","text":"Fix and lock it in"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-bot-that-stopped-finding-the-policy","text":"Incident: The bot that stopped finding the policy"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Reduce latency","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-latency"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Errors","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/api/errors"}],"text":"When an LLM feature misbehaves there is no stack trace. The system is probabilistic and the failure may be in retrieval, the prompt, the model, the limits or the code around it. A fixed order of checks finds the cause quickly and turns each incident into a permanent test. What you will learn Capture the exact request and response Check limits, stop reasons and errors Separate retrieval, prompt, model and parsing problems Reproduce, fix and add a regression test Capture everything You cannot debug what you cannot see. For every request, log the request ID, model and version, the full assembled prompt (with personal data protected), parameters, retrieved passages, tool calls and results, the raw reply, the stop reason, token counts and latency. Store them for a limited time with access controls. A trace that connects one user report to one stored request is worth more than any guess. First checks jq &#x27;{id, model, stop_reason, usage, error}&#x27; logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json jq -r &#x27;.prompt&#x27; logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json | head -60 jq -r &#x27;.reply&#x27; logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json Look for: an error or a 429/529 ; stop_reason other than a normal end (cut off by length); the model version changed; the prompt contains stale or missing text; a very large input that pushed out instructions; the reply that is valid text but fails your parser. Which layer failed? Retrieval or context : is the needed fact in the prompt? If not, fix search, chunking or the data. The model cannot use what it never saw. Prompt : is the instruction clear, are examples consistent, do two rules conflict? Try the same input with a simplified prompt. Model : with the right facts and a clear prompt, does it still fail? Try a stronger model or break the task into steps. Parsing and code : is the output valid but your code mishandles it, for example by assuming the first content block is text? Environment : did a tool fail silently, or return an error that the model had to guess around? Fix and lock it in Reproduce with the stored request, change one thing, and re-run. Then add the case to the evaluation set with the expected behaviour, so it cannot return unnoticed. Compare the new pass rate with the old on the whole set, not only on the case you fixed. Record the cause in your runbook: most incidents repeat. Add monitors for the signals that would have caught this earlier, such as parse-failure rate, empty-answer rate, refusal rate, token growth and user thumbs-down rate. Predict first. A support bot suddenly says \"I don't have that information\" for questions it answered yesterday. Which layer do you check first? Your guess Answer: Retrieval and context: check whether the relevant document is still being found and included, before blaming the model. Is the fact in the prompt? How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Debugging LLM applications fits together Flow in order: 1, Capture everything to First checks; 2, First checks to Which layer failed?; 3, Which layer failed? to Fix and lock it in. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Capture everything Step What it does: You cannot debug what you cannot see. For every request, log the request ID, model and version, the full assembled prompt (with personal data protected), parameters... If it fails or is weak: Blaming the model before checking the prompt contents. First checks Observability What it does: Look for: an error or a 429/529; stop_reason other than a normal end (cut off by length); the model version changed; the prompt contains stale or missing text; a very... If it fails or is weak: Not logging the assembled prompt. Which layer failed? Step What it does: Retrieval or context: is the needed fact in the prompt? If it fails or is"},"llm/embeddings-and-vector-search":{"html":"\u003cp>Keyword search fails when people use different words for the same idea. Embeddings represent meaning as numbers, which lets you search by similarity. It is the base of retrieval for language model applications.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What an embedding is and how similarity works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Chunking documents and storing vectors with metadata\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Approximate nearest neighbor indexes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hybrid search and evaluating retrieval\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"vectors-and-similarity\">Vectors and similarity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import numpy as np\n\ndef cosine(a, b):\n    return float(np.dot(a, b) / (np.linalg.norm(a) * np.linalg.norm(b)))\n\ndocs = {&quot;reset password&quot;: [0.9, 0.1, 0.0], &quot;change billing card&quot;: [0.1, 0.9, 0.1]}\nquery = [0.85, 0.2, 0.05]            # &quot;I forgot my login&quot;\nranked = sorted(docs, key=lambda k: cosine(query, docs[k]), reverse=True)\nprint(ranked)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> numpy \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> np\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">cosine\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">a, b\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">float\u003c/span>(np.dot(a, b) / (np.linalg.norm(a) * np.linalg.norm(b)))\n\ndocs = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;reset password&quot;\u003c/span>: [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.9\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.1\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.0\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;change billing card&quot;\u003c/span>: [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.1\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.9\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.1\u003c/span>]}\nquery = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.85\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.05\u003c/span>]            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># &quot;I forgot my login&quot;\u003c/span>\nranked = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sorted\u003c/span>(docs, key=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda\u003c/span> k: cosine(query, docs[k]), reverse=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(ranked)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An embedding model returns a vector of hundreds or thousands of numbers. Texts with similar meaning get vectors that point in similar directions, and \u003cstrong>cosine similarity\u003c/strong> measures that angle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"chunking-and-metadata\">Chunking and metadata\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Split documents into pieces of a few hundred tokens along natural boundaries such as headings, with a small overlap. Store each chunk with metadata: source, section, date, access group and version. Metadata lets you filter, cite and enforce permissions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"indexes\">Indexes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Comparing a query with millions of vectors is slow, so databases use \u003cstrong>approximate nearest neighbor\u003c/strong> indexes such as HNSW or IVF. They trade a little recall for large speed gains. Tune the search depth and measure recall against an exact search on a sample.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hybrid-and-evaluation\">Hybrid and evaluation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pure vector search can miss exact terms like error codes and product names. \u003cstrong>Hybrid search\u003c/strong> combines keyword and vector scores. Build a small test set of real queries with known right documents and measure whether the right chunk appears in the top results, before and after each change to chunking or the model.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Embeddings and vector search fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Vectors and similarity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Chunking and metadata\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Indexes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Hybrid and evaluation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Vectors and similarity to Chunking and metadata; 2, Chunking and metadata to Indexes; 3, Indexes to Hybrid and evaluation.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Vectors and similarity \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An embedding model returns a vector of hundreds or thousands of numbers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using only vector search for codes and names.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Chunking and metadata \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Split documents into pieces of a few hundred tokens along natural boundaries such as headings, with a small overlap.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No metadata or access filters.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Indexes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Comparing a query with millions of vectors is slow, so databases use approximate nearest neighbor indexes such as HNSW or IVF.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Chunks too large or cut mid-sentence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Hybrid and evaluation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pure vector search can miss exact terms like error codes and product names.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-right-document-ranked-tenth\">Incident: The right document ranked tenth\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A support assistant could not find an answer that existed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">query: &quot;ERR-4031 payment declined&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">top result: general payment FAQ\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">expected page ERR-4031 troubleshooting: rank 10\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Vector search treated the error code as a rare word and returned general text. Adding keyword search for exact matches and boosting title matches put the right page first, and a test set of real tickets guarded against future regressions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using only vector search for codes and names\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Chunks too large or cut mid-sentence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No metadata or access filters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never measuring retrieval quality\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"vectors-and-similarity","text":"Vectors and similarity"},{"id":"chunking-and-metadata","text":"Chunking and metadata"},{"id":"indexes","text":"Indexes"},{"id":"hybrid-and-evaluation","text":"Hybrid and evaluation"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-right-document-ranked-tenth","text":"Incident: The right document ranked tenth"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic embeddings guide","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/embeddings"},{"t":"Hugging Face sentence embeddings","u":"https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/index"}],"text":"Keyword search fails when people use different words for the same idea. Embeddings represent meaning as numbers, which lets you search by similarity. It is the base of retrieval for language model applications. What you will learn What an embedding is and how similarity works Chunking documents and storing vectors with metadata Approximate nearest neighbor indexes Hybrid search and evaluating retrieval Vectors and similarity import numpy as np def cosine ( a, b ): return float (np.dot(a, b) / (np.linalg.norm(a) * np.linalg.norm(b))) docs = { \"reset password\" : [ 0.9 , 0.1 , 0.0 ], \"change billing card\" : [ 0.1 , 0.9 , 0.1 ]} query = [ 0.85 , 0.2 , 0.05 ] # \"I forgot my login\" ranked = sorted (docs, key= lambda k: cosine(query, docs[k]), reverse= True ) print (ranked) An embedding model returns a vector of hundreds or thousands of numbers. Texts with similar meaning get vectors that point in similar directions, and cosine similarity measures that angle. Chunking and metadata Split documents into pieces of a few hundred tokens along natural boundaries such as headings, with a small overlap. Store each chunk with metadata: source, section, date, access group and version. Metadata lets you filter, cite and enforce permissions. Indexes Comparing a query with millions of vectors is slow, so databases use approximate nearest neighbor indexes such as HNSW or IVF. They trade a little recall for large speed gains. Tune the search depth and measure recall against an exact search on a sample. Hybrid and evaluation Pure vector search can miss exact terms like error codes and product names. Hybrid search combines keyword and vector scores. Build a small test set of real queries with known right documents and measure whether the right chunk appears in the top results, before and after each change to chunking or the model. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Embeddings and vector search fits together Flow in order: 1, Vectors and similarity to Chunking and metadata; 2, Chunking and metadata to Indexes; 3, Indexes to Hybrid and evaluation. Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Vectors and similarity Step What it does: An embedding model returns a vector of hundreds or thousands of numbers. If it fails or is weak: Using only vector search for codes and names. Chunking and metadata Data store What it does: Split documents into pieces of a few hundred tokens along natural boundaries such as headings, with a small overlap. If it fails or is weak: No metadata or access filters. Indexes Data store What it does: Comparing a query with millions of vectors is slow, so databases use approximate nearest neighbor indexes such as HNSW or IVF. If it fails or is weak: Chunks too large or cut mid-sentence. Hybrid and evaluation Step What it does: Pure vector search can miss exact terms like error codes and product names. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Incident: The right document ranked tenth Incident A support assistant could not find an answer that existed. query: \"ERR-4031 payment declined\" top result: general payment FAQ expected page ERR-4031 troubleshooting: rank 10 Vector search treated the error code as a rare word and returned general text. Adding keyword search for exact matches and boosting title matches put the right page first, and a test set of real tickets guarded against future regressions. Common mistakes Using only vector search for codes and names Chunks too large or cut mid-sentence No metadata or access filters Never measuring retrieval quality"},"llm/retrieval-augmented-generation":{"html":"\u003cp>A language model knows what was in its training data and nothing about your wiki, tickets or last week&#39;s decisions. Retrieval-augmented generation bridges that gap without retraining.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The RAG pipeline: ingest, retrieve, generate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prompting for grounded, cited answers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handling no results and conflicting sources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Evaluating retrieval and answers separately\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-pipeline\">The pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Ingest:\u003c/strong> load documents, clean them, chunk them, embed them and store them with metadata.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Retrieve:\u003c/strong> embed the question, find the top chunks, and optionally rerank them.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Generate:\u003c/strong> build a prompt with the chunks and ask the model to answer from them.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def rag_answer(question, user, search, llm):\n    chunks = search(question, top_k=6, filter={&quot;groups&quot;: user.groups})\n    if not chunks:\n        return &quot;I could not find this in the documentation.&quot;\n    context = &quot;\\n\\n&quot;.join(f&quot;[{c.id}] {c.text}&quot; for c in chunks)\n    prompt = (\n        &quot;Use only the context to answer. Cite the ids you used like [id]. &quot;\n        &quot;If the context does not answer the question, say so.\\n\\n&quot;\n        f&quot;Context:\\n{context}\\n\\nQuestion: {question}&quot;\n    )\n    return llm(prompt)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">rag_answer\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">question, user, search, llm\u003c/span>):\n    chunks = search(question, top_k=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">filter\u003c/span>={\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;groups&quot;\u003c/span>: user.groups})\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> chunks:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;I could not find this in the documentation.&quot;\u003c/span>\n    context = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\\n\\n&quot;\u003c/span>.join(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{c.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>}\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{c.text}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> chunks)\n    prompt = (\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Use only the context to answer. Cite the ids you used like [id]. &quot;\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;If the context does not answer the question, say so.\\n\\n&quot;\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;Context:\\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{context}\u003c/span>\\n\\nQuestion: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{question}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n    )\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> llm(prompt)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"grounded-answers\">Grounded answers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tell the model to use only the context, cite sources and admit gaps. Put the context in a clearly marked section. Handle conflicts by preferring newer or higher-authority documents, and show dates.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access-control\">Access control\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Apply permissions at \u003cstrong>retrieval time\u003c/strong> with metadata filters, not only in the prompt. A model cannot keep a secret that you put in its context.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"evaluate-in-two-parts\">Evaluate in two parts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Measure \u003cstrong>retrieval\u003c/strong> (did the right chunk appear in the top results) and \u003cstrong>generation\u003c/strong> (is the answer correct and faithful to the chunks). When quality drops, this tells you whether to improve chunking, search or prompts. Keep a regression set and update documents on a schedule, since stale sources create confident wrong answers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Grounded answers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Access control\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Evaluate in two parts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The pipeline to Grounded answers; 2, Grounded answers to Access control; 3, Access control to Evaluate in two parts.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The pipeline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ingest: load documents, clean them, chunk them, embed them and store them with metadata.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Enforcing access only in the prompt.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Grounded answers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tell the model to use only the context, cite sources and admit gaps.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Measuring only final answers, not retrieval.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Access control \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Apply permissions at retrieval time with metadata filters, not only in the prompt.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A model cannot keep a secret that you put in its context.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Evaluate in two parts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Measure retrieval (did the right chunk appear in the top results) and generation (is the answer correct and faithful to the chunks).\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No answer when nothing relevant is found.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-assistant-that-quoted-a-confidential-file\">Incident: The assistant that quoted a confidential file\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Retrieval ignored permissions and exposed a restricted document.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">user: contractor\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">retrieved: finance/salary-bands.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">answer included salary ranges\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The index held every document and relied on the prompt to keep secrets. A contractor&#39;s question retrieved a restricted file. The team added group metadata and filtered at query time, tested access with sample users and logged which documents were used.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enforcing access only in the prompt\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No answer when nothing relevant is found\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never refreshing stale documents\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring only final answers, not retrieval\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-pipeline","text":"The pipeline"},{"id":"grounded-answers","text":"Grounded answers"},{"id":"access-control","text":"Access control"},{"id":"evaluate-in-two-parts","text":"Evaluate in two parts"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-assistant-that-quoted-a-confidential-file","text":"Incident: The assistant that quoted a confidential file"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic: retrieval and contextual embeddings","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/embeddings"},{"t":"Anthropic prompt engineering overview","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview"}],"text":"A language model knows what was in its training data and nothing about your wiki, tickets or last week&#39;s decisions. Retrieval-augmented generation bridges that gap without retraining. What you will learn The RAG pipeline: ingest, retrieve, generate Prompting for grounded, cited answers Handling no results and conflicting sources Evaluating retrieval and answers separately The pipeline Ingest: load documents, clean them, chunk them, embed them and store them with metadata. Retrieve: embed the question, find the top chunks, and optionally rerank them. Generate: build a prompt with the chunks and ask the model to answer from them. def rag_answer ( question, user, search, llm ): chunks = search(question, top_k= 6 , filter ={ \"groups\" : user.groups}) if not chunks: return \"I could not find this in the documentation.\" context = \"\\n\\n\" .join( f\"[ {c. id } ] {c.text} \" for c in chunks) prompt = ( \"Use only the context to answer. Cite the ids you used like [id]. \" \"If the context does not answer the question, say so.\\n\\n\" f\"Context:\\n {context} \\n\\nQuestion: {question} \" ) return llm(prompt) Grounded answers Tell the model to use only the context, cite sources and admit gaps. Put the context in a clearly marked section. Handle conflicts by preferring newer or higher-authority documents, and show dates. Access control Apply permissions at retrieval time with metadata filters, not only in the prompt. A model cannot keep a secret that you put in its context. Evaluate in two parts Measure retrieval (did the right chunk appear in the top results) and generation (is the answer correct and faithful to the chunks). When quality drops, this tells you whether to improve chunking, search or prompts. Keep a regression set and update documents on a schedule, since stale sources create confident wrong answers. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) fits together Flow in order: 1, The pipeline to Grounded answers; 2, Grounded answers to Access control; 3, Access control to Evaluate in two parts. Queue Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The pipeline Queue What it does: Ingest: load documents, clean them, chunk them, embed them and store them with metadata. If it fails or is weak: Enforcing access only in the prompt. Grounded answers Step What it does: Tell the model to use only the context, cite sources and admit gaps. If it fails or is weak: Measuring only final answers, not retrieval. Access control Security What it does: Apply permissions at retrieval time with metadata filters, not only in the prompt. If it fails or is weak: A model cannot keep a secret that you put in its context. Evaluate in two parts Step What it does: Measure retrieval (did the right chunk appear in the top results) and generation (is the answer correct and faithful to the chunks). If it fails or is weak: No answer when nothing relevant is found. Incident: The assistant that quoted a confidential file Incident Retrieval ignored permissions and exposed a restricted document. user: contractor retrieved: finance/salary-bands.md answer included salary ranges The index held every document and relied on the prompt to keep secrets. A contractor&#39;s question retrieved a restricted file. The team added group metadata and filtered at query time, tested access with sample users and logged which documents were used. Common mistakes Enforcing access only in the prompt No answer when nothing relevant is found Never refreshing stale documents Measuring only final answers, not retrieval"},"llm/fine-tuning-versus-prompting-versus-rag":{"html":"\u003cp>There are three main ways to improve a language model application, and people often reach for the most expensive one first. A short diagnosis of the failure tells you which to use.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What each approach changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Diagnosing the failure type\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cost, risk and maintenance of each\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A sensible order of attempts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-each-one-changes\">What each one changes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prompting:\u003c/strong> instructions, examples, format and constraints. Fast, cheap and reversible.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>RAG:\u003c/strong> the knowledge the model sees. Good for private, large or changing facts, and for citations.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Fine-tuning:\u003c/strong> the model&#39;s behavior. Good for a consistent style, strict format or a narrow task where examples are plentiful.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"diagnose-first\">Diagnose first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Symptom\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Likely fix\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Ignores format or instructions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Better prompt, examples, structured output\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Wrong or outdated facts about your data\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>RAG\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Needs a house style at scale\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Examples first, then fine-tuning\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Too slow or costly for a simple task\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Smaller model, possibly fine-tuned\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Makes things up\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Grounding, citations, allowing &quot;I do not know&quot;\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# a tiny harness: run the same cases against each variant\ncases = load_jsonl(&quot;evals/cases.jsonl&quot;)\nfor name, system in [(&quot;prompt-v1&quot;, p1), (&quot;prompt-v2&quot;, p2), (&quot;rag&quot;, rag), (&quot;tuned&quot;, tuned)]:\n    score = sum(grade(system(c.input), c.expected) for c in cases) / len(cases)\n    print(name, round(score, 3))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># a tiny harness: run the same cases against each variant\u003c/span>\ncases = load_jsonl(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;evals/cases.jsonl&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> name, system \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> [(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prompt-v1&quot;\u003c/span>, p1), (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prompt-v2&quot;\u003c/span>, p2), (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rag&quot;\u003c/span>, rag), (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tuned&quot;\u003c/span>, tuned)]:\n    score = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(grade(system(c.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">input\u003c/span>), c.expected) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> cases) / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(cases)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(name, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(score, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-and-maintenance\">Cost and maintenance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Fine-tuning needs good labeled data, a training and evaluation pipeline, and retraining when requirements or base models change. A tuned model can also lose general abilities. RAG needs a data pipeline and search quality work, but updates are immediate when documents change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"order-of-attempts\">Order of attempts\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Write an evaluation set from real examples.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Improve the prompt and examples.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add retrieval if facts are the problem.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Consider fine-tuning only for a measured gap, with enough clean data.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Prompting, RAG or fine-tuning: choosing fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What each one changes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Diagnose first\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cost and maintenance\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Order of attempts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, What each one changes to Diagnose first; 2, Diagnose first to Cost and maintenance; 3, Cost and maintenance to Order of attempts.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>What each one changes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Prompting: instructions, examples, format and constraints.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fine-tuning to teach changing facts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Diagnose first \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> .\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fine-tuning with a small, messy dataset.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Cost and maintenance \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Fine-tuning needs good labeled data, a training and evaluation pipeline, and retraining when requirements or base models change.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No evaluation set before changing anything.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Order of attempts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Write an evaluation set from real examples.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping prompt improvements.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-fine-tuned-on-yesterday-s-prices\">Incident: Fine-tuned on yesterday&#39;s prices\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A tuned model gave outdated answers because knowledge was baked into the weights.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">question: price of plan Pro?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">answer: $29/month\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">current price (source of truth): $39/month\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team had put changing facts into training data. Every price change made the model wrong until the next training run. They moved facts to retrieval from the pricing service and kept fine-tuning only for tone and output format.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Fine-tuning to teach changing facts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No evaluation set before changing anything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping prompt improvements\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fine-tuning with a small, messy dataset\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-each-one-changes","text":"What each one changes"},{"id":"diagnose-first","text":"Diagnose first"},{"id":"cost-and-maintenance","text":"Cost and maintenance"},{"id":"order-of-attempts","text":"Order of attempts"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-fine-tuned-on-yesterday-s-prices","text":"Incident: Fine-tuned on yesterday's prices"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic prompt engineering overview","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview"},{"t":"Anthropic: define success criteria","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/define-success"}],"text":"There are three main ways to improve a language model application, and people often reach for the most expensive one first. A short diagnosis of the failure tells you which to use. What you will learn What each approach changes Diagnosing the failure type Cost, risk and maintenance of each A sensible order of attempts What each one changes Prompting: instructions, examples, format and constraints. Fast, cheap and reversible. RAG: the knowledge the model sees. Good for private, large or changing facts, and for citations. Fine-tuning: the model&#39;s behavior. Good for a consistent style, strict format or a narrow task where examples are plentiful. Diagnose first Symptom Likely fix Ignores format or instructions Better prompt, examples, structured output Wrong or outdated facts about your data RAG Needs a house style at scale Examples first, then fine-tuning Too slow or costly for a simple task Smaller model, possibly fine-tuned Makes things up Grounding, citations, allowing \"I do not know\" # a tiny harness: run the same cases against each variant cases = load_jsonl( \"evals/cases.jsonl\" ) for name, system in [( \"prompt-v1\" , p1), ( \"prompt-v2\" , p2), ( \"rag\" , rag), ( \"tuned\" , tuned)]: score = sum (grade(system(c. input ), c.expected) for c in cases) / len (cases) print (name, round (score, 3 )) Cost and maintenance Fine-tuning needs good labeled data, a training and evaluation pipeline, and retraining when requirements or base models change. A tuned model can also lose general abilities. RAG needs a data pipeline and search quality work, but updates are immediate when documents change. Order of attempts Write an evaluation set from real examples. Improve the prompt and examples. Add retrieval if facts are the problem. Consider fine-tuning only for a measured gap, with enough clean data. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Prompting, RAG or fine-tuning: choosing fits together Flow in order: 1, What each one changes to Diagnose first; 2, Diagnose first to Cost and maintenance; 3, Cost and maintenance to Order of attempts. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. What each one changes Step What it does: Prompting: instructions, examples, format and constraints. If it fails or is weak: Fine-tuning to teach changing facts. Diagnose first Step What it does: . If it fails or is weak: Fine-tuning with a small, messy dataset. Cost and maintenance Step What it does: Fine-tuning needs good labeled data, a training and evaluation pipeline, and retraining when requirements or base models change. If it fails or is weak: No evaluation set before changing anything. Order of attempts Step What it does: Write an evaluation set from real examples. If it fails or is weak: Skipping prompt improvements. Incident: Fine-tuned on yesterday&#39;s prices Incident A tuned model gave outdated answers because knowledge was baked into the weights. question: price of plan Pro? answer: $29/month current price (source of truth): $39/month The team had put changing facts into training data. Every price change made the model wrong until the next training run. They moved facts to retrieval from the pricing service and kept fine-tuning only for tone and output format. Common mistakes Fine-tuning to teach changing facts No evaluation set before changing anything Skipping prompt improvements Fine-tuning with a small, messy dataset"},"llm/llm-safety-privacy-and-data-handling":{"html":"\u003cp>Language model applications mix untrusted text, private data and powerful tools. Most incidents come from a few predictable mistakes: sending too much, logging too much and trusting text that came from outside.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Classifying and redacting data before it reaches a model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Provider settings: retention, training and region\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prompt injection and untrusted content\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe logging and output handling\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"data-in-prompts\">Data in prompts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Decide which classes of data may go to which provider. Redact secrets, tokens and personal identifiers before sending, replacing them with placeholders and restoring them afterwards if needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import re\n\nPATTERNS = {\n    &quot;email&quot;: re.compile(r&quot;[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[\\w.]+&quot;),\n    &quot;token&quot;: re.compile(r&quot;\\b(?:sk|ghp|AKIA)[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}\\b&quot;),\n}\n\ndef redact(text):\n    for name, rx in PATTERNS.items():\n        text = rx.sub(f&quot;[{name.upper()}]&quot;, text)\n    return text\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> re\n\nPATTERNS = {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;email&quot;\u003c/span>: re.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">compile\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">r&quot;[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[\\w.]+&quot;\u003c/span>),\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;token&quot;\u003c/span>: re.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">compile\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">r&quot;\\b(?:sk|ghp|AKIA)[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}\\b&quot;\u003c/span>),\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">redact\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">text\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> name, rx \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> PATTERNS.items():\n        text = rx.sub(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{name.upper()}\u003c/span>]&quot;\u003c/span>, text)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> text\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"provider-and-retention\">Provider and retention\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check contract terms: whether inputs are used for training, how long they are retained, where they are processed and what audit options exist. Use zero or short retention for sensitive workloads and keep a record of approved providers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prompt-injection\">Prompt injection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Text from emails, web pages and documents can contain instructions that try to hijack the model. Treat all such content as \u003cstrong>data, not commands\u003c/strong>: put it in a delimited section, tell the model not to follow instructions inside it, and, more importantly, limit what the application can do. If a model reads untrusted text, do not give it the ability to send email, move money or read secrets without a human approval step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logging-and-outputs\">Logging and outputs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Logs help debugging and can leak data. Redact before logging, restrict access and set retention. Validate model output before using it: escape it before displaying, check JSON against a schema and never execute generated code or SQL without review and sandboxing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How LLM safety, privacy and data handling fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Data in prompts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Provider and retention\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Prompt injection\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Logging and outputs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Data in prompts to Provider and retention; 2, Provider and retention to Prompt injection; 3, Prompt injection to Logging and outputs.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Data in prompts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Decide which classes of data may go to which provider.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Logging full prompts forever.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Provider and retention \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Check contract terms: whether inputs are used for training, how long they are retained, where they are processed and what audit options exist.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping provider retention checks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Prompt injection \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Text from emails, web pages and documents can contain instructions that try to hijack the model.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Giving a model that reads untrusted text powerful tools.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Logging and outputs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Logs help debugging and can leak data. Redact before logging, restrict access and set retention.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using output without validation.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-customer-emails-in-the-debug-logs\">Incident: Customer emails in the debug logs\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Prompt logging stored private data with weak access controls.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">log: prompt=&quot;Hi, my card 4111 1111 1111 1111 declined, call 555-0143&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">retention: unlimited\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">readers: all engineering (412 people)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The debug logging was enabled to improve prompts and was never reviewed. Logs held card numbers and phone numbers. The team redacted before logging, limited access to a small group, set a 14-day retention and added data checks to code review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logging full prompts forever\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving a model that reads untrusted text powerful tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping provider retention checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using output without validation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"data-in-prompts","text":"Data in prompts"},{"id":"provider-and-retention","text":"Provider and retention"},{"id":"prompt-injection","text":"Prompt injection"},{"id":"logging-and-outputs","text":"Logging and outputs"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-customer-emails-in-the-debug-logs","text":"Incident: Customer emails in the debug logs"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OWASP Top 10 for LLM applications","u":"https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/"},{"t":"NIST AI risk management framework","u":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}],"text":"Language model applications mix untrusted text, private data and powerful tools. Most incidents come from a few predictable mistakes: sending too much, logging too much and trusting text that came from outside. What you will learn Classifying and redacting data before it reaches a model Provider settings: retention, training and region Prompt injection and untrusted content Safe logging and output handling Data in prompts Decide which classes of data may go to which provider. Redact secrets, tokens and personal identifiers before sending, replacing them with placeholders and restoring them afterwards if needed. import re PATTERNS = { \"email\" : re. compile ( r\"[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[\\w.]+\" ), \"token\" : re. compile ( r\"\\b(?:sk|ghp|AKIA)[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}\\b\" ), } def redact ( text ): for name, rx in PATTERNS.items(): text = rx.sub( f\"[ {name.upper()} ]\" , text) return text Provider and retention Check contract terms: whether inputs are used for training, how long they are retained, where they are processed and what audit options exist. Use zero or short retention for sensitive workloads and keep a record of approved providers. Prompt injection Text from emails, web pages and documents can contain instructions that try to hijack the model. Treat all such content as data, not commands : put it in a delimited section, tell the model not to follow instructions inside it, and, more importantly, limit what the application can do. If a model reads untrusted text, do not give it the ability to send email, move money or read secrets without a human approval step. Logging and outputs Logs help debugging and can leak data. Redact before logging, restrict access and set retention. Validate model output before using it: escape it before displaying, check JSON against a schema and never execute generated code or SQL without review and sandboxing. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How LLM safety, privacy and data handling fits together Flow in order: 1, Data in prompts to Provider and retention; 2, Provider and retention to Prompt injection; 3, Prompt injection to Logging and outputs. Data store Outside party Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Data in prompts Data store What it does: Decide which classes of data may go to which provider. If it fails or is weak: Logging full prompts forever. Provider and retention Outside party What it does: Check contract terms: whether inputs are used for training, how long they are retained, where they are processed and what audit options exist. If it fails or is weak: Skipping provider retention checks. Prompt injection Step What it does: Text from emails, web pages and documents can contain instructions that try to hijack the model. If it fails or is weak: Giving a model that reads untrusted text powerful tools. Logging and outputs Observability What it does: Logs help debugging and can leak data. Redact before logging, restrict access and set retention. If it fails or is weak: Using output without validation. Incident: Customer emails in the debug logs Incident Prompt logging stored private data with weak access controls. log: prompt=\"Hi, my card 4111 1111 1111 1111 declined, call 555-0143\" retention: unlimited readers: all engineering (412 people) The debug logging was enabled to improve prompts and was never reviewed. Logs held card numbers and phone numbers. The team redacted before logging, limited access to a small group, set a 14-day retention and added data checks to code review. Common mistakes Logging full prompts forever Giving a model that reads untrusted text powerful tools Skipping provider retention checks Using output without validation"}});